5. CONTENT CHANGES MUST REACH THE REAL WEBSITE

This requirement is critical.

Saving content inside the Android application must not merely update local Android state.

The content must be written through the project's configured and authorized backend, database, or content API.

The actual website must retrieve the updated content through its configured data source.

Use an architecture such as:

Android Master Admin
|
v
Authenticated Backend API
|
v
Authorized Project Integration
|
v
Database / CMS / Existing Content API
|
v
Website reads the updated content

This is an architectural example, not a requirement to force every project into the same database.

Where a website already has a working backend or CMS, integrate with it instead of unnecessarily replacing it.

Where a project has no suitable backend, provide a migration or setup workflow to add one.

Where a website currently stores content only in React state, local storage, or hardcoded source files, identify that limitation and propose the appropriate persistent solution.

Provide refresh, synchronization status, and error handling.

Do not report a successful save until the actual configured backend confirms success.

If the backend write succeeds but the website has not yet refreshed, clearly distinguish “Saved” from “Visible on website”.

Support optimistic UI updates only when they can be rolled back on failure.

